  "query_preview": "(Today through Friday)...\nIssued at 211 AM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nA strong upper low will pivot from the eastern Great Lakes into \nthe Canadian Maritimes over the weekend...gradually loosening its \ngrip on the weather across the region. Dry and seasonably cool \nweather is expected through early next week but as broad ridging \naloft and at the surface expands across the Ohio Valley... \ntemperatures will warm as the week progresses.\n\nToday through Sunday Night\n\nAreas of low stratus continue to redevelop across primarily the \nnortheastern half of the forecast area early this morning. Despite \nthese clouds...temperatures had fallen in to the lower and mid 30s \nacross most of central Indiana as of 07Z.\n\nThe eastward progression of the upper low into New England today \nwill enable drier air to begin advecting into the Ohio Valley but \nan overall cyclonic flow aloft will persist on the back side of the \nparent upper trough. A more expansive broken stratocu deck will \nredevelop as a result later this morning but should largely remain \nconfined to the northeast half of the forecast area downwind of the \nLake Michigan fetch with scattered diurnal cu elsewhere. Low clouds \nwill linger across northeast counties tonight with skies clearing \nelsewhere as a strong high pressure ridge expands into the area from \nthe west. The overall trend for Sunday will be for increasing \nsunshine and less clouds as the center of the surface high drifts \ninto the mid Mississippi Valley. Stratocu should be more cellular in \nnature on Sunday afternoon across northeast counties as \nprogressively drier air spreads east. Skies will become mostly clear \nacross the entire forecast area Sunday night as high pressure \ncenters over the region. \n\nTemps...highs today will range from the mid 40s northeast where \ngreater cloud cover is expected to the lower 50s in the lower\nWabash Valley. Low level thermals suggest slightly warmer \ntemperatures Sunday. Lows will range from the mid 20s to lower 30s\nboth tonight and Sunday night.\n\nMonday through Friday\n\nWith the departure of the upper trough off to the east...there will \nbe a transition to broad ridging aloft that will persist for much of \nthe upcoming week. The aforementioned surface high pressure will \nsettle across the eastern half of the country with progressively \ndeeper subsidence across the region keeping skies mostly clear. Any \npotential for rain will hold off until next weekend at the earliest \nat this point. The onset of warm advection will usher in a slow \nwarming trend with highs rising into the 60s for the second half of \nnext week.",
